        "original_id": "fc2c2c5b-bd4c-5c99-a3a4-fd6d4b5185ac",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Antelope, Kansas\n- Species: Pronghorn\n- Concepts: Naming origins, colloquial names, parallel evolution\n\nFacts:\n- Antelope, Kansas, was named after antelope seen grazing near the site where the first school was being built.\n- The pronghorn, Antilocapra americana, is an artiodactyl mammal species indigenous to interior western and central North America.\n- The pronghorn is not a true antelope, but in North America it is colloquially called the American antelope, prong buck, pronghorn antelope, or simply antelope.\n- The pronghorn resembles the true antelopes of the Old World and occupies a similar ecological niche because of parallel evolution.",
